1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,340
In the summer of 1518,

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:06,940
people in Strasbourg began dancing uncontrollably.

3
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:10,880
Contemporary and later accounts describe a troubling

4
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:12,938
outbreak that baffled the city.

5
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:16,160
Reports describe more people joining,

6
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:18,700
some apparently unable to stop.

7
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:21,700
The numbers and exact details vary in

8
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:25,040
the sources, but the episode became notorious.

9
00:00:25,891 --> 00:00:29,920
Authorities tried measures that included organizing space

10
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:31,620
and musicians for the dancers.

11
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:35,320
Those choices reflected theories about the illness

12
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:37,082
that we would not share today.

13
00:00:38,440 --> 00:00:41,220
Chroniclers reported exhaustion and suffering.

14
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:44,320
Claims about large numbers of deaths are

15
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:47,860
much less securely documented than popular retellings

16
00:00:47,860 --> 00:00:48,600
suggest.

17
00:00:49,406 --> 00:00:52,420
Modern historians have considered stress,

18
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:56,180
belief, disease and social contagion.

19
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:58,680
No single cause has been proved from

20
00:00:58,680 --> 00:00:59,960
the surviving evidence.

21
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,160
What remains is a city confronting behavior

22
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:05,560
it could not control,

23
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:09,380
while leaders acted without understanding its mechanism.

24
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:12,420
The strangest part may be the response.
